# TSHARK & WIRESHARK UTILS
Nixpkgs: wireshark
Source: https://www.wireshark.org
Description: Terminal-based network protocol analyzer suite
Version: 4.6.4

## Commands

### Basics
- tshark -D: list available network interfaces
- tshark -i [interface]: capture packets on a specific interface
- tshark -r [file].pcap: read packets from a pcap file
- capinfos [file].pcap: print detailed statistics and info about a capture file
- captype [file].pcap: print the file format type of a capture file

### Essentials
- tshark -f "tcp port 80": apply a capture filter (BPF syntax) before capturing
- tshark -Y "http.request": apply a display filter (Wireshark syntax)
- dumpcap -i [interface] -w [file].pcap: high-performance raw packet capture
- editcap -d [input].pcap [output].pcap: remove duplicate packets from a file
- mergecap -w [output].pcap [file1].pcap [file2].pcap: merge multiple files into one

### Intermediaries
- tshark -T fields -e frame.time -e ip.src -e ip.dst: extract specific packet fields
- reordercap [input].pcap [output].pcap: reorder packets by timestamp
- text2pcap [text_file] [pcap_file]: convert an ASCII hex dump to a pcap file
- dftest "http.request": test and verify a Wireshark display filter syntax
- mmdbresolve -f [db_file] -i [ip_addr]: resolve IP addresses using a MaxMind database

### Advanceds
- rawshark -r [file].pcap: analyze raw pcap data from a pipe or file
- sharkd: run the Wireshark engine as a daemon for remote RPC analysis
- editcap -c 1000 [input].pcap [output_prefix].pcap: split a large file into smaller 1000-packet chunks
- tshark -z io,phs: display protocol hierarchy statistics

### Uncommons
- randpkt -c 10 -t dns [file].pcap: generate a pcap file with random DNS packets
- idl2wrs [file].idl: generate a Wireshark dissector from a CORBA IDL file

## Tips
- use -n in tshark to avoid slow DNS lookups during live captures
- combine tools: tshark -r file.pcap -T json | jq
- Android workflow: use PcapDroid to capture traffic without root (via VPN service), save as .pcap, then analyze in Termux/PRoot using tshark/capinfos
- Android PcapDroid Streaming: use 'UDP Streaming' in PcapDroid and capture in Termux with 'tshark -i [port]'

## Disclaimers
- direct interface capture requires root/sudo; on Android, root is required for tshark -i
- respect privacy and local regulations when monitoring network traffic

## Alternatives
- tcpdump: lower-level, classic packet sniffer
- termshark: TUI wrapper for tshark

